package cajeta.io.net;

/**
 * A bidirectional **async byte channel** — the transport seam the buffered
 * [`AsyncReader`](AsyncReader.cajeta) / [`AsyncWriter`](AsyncWriter.cajeta)
 * (and, through them, the HTTP/1.1 and WebSocket codecs) read and write
 * *through*, abstracting **plaintext vs TLS**.
 *
 * The two implementors are:
 *
 *   - [`TcpStream`](TcpStream.cajeta) — the raw socket (plaintext `http://` /
 *     `ws://`), where `readAsync` / `writeAllAsync` are the reactor-parking
 *     non-blocking ops directly;
 *   - [`TlsStream`](tls/TlsStream.cajeta) — the TLS engine over a socket
 *     (`https://` / `wss://`), where the same calls drive the memory-BIO
 *     pump (encrypt-on-write, decrypt-on-read) over the socket underneath.
 *
 * Because both satisfy this one interface, the **same** buffered reader/
 * writer — and therefore the **same** `dev.cajeta.http` `HttpServer`
 * connection loop and [`HttpClient`](http/HttpClient.cajeta) request path —
 * run unchanged over a cleartext or an encrypted transport (plan items
 * **NET-8.1** client / **NET-9.5** HTTPS server). This is the keystone seam
 * that lets TLS termination be a *wrapping* concern, not a fork of the
 * protocol code.
 *
 * ## Contract
 *
 *   - [`readAsync`](#readAsync) returns the count read (`>= 0`); `0` is an
 *     orderly peer close (EOF) — the terminate-on-`0` contract the buffered
 *     reader drives. It **parks the fiber** (not the carrier) until data is
 *     ready.
 *   - [`writeAllAsync`](#writeAllAsync) sends every requested byte, parking
 *     the fiber on back-pressure; it raises on a broken peer.
 *   - [`readWithin`](#readWithin) is the deadline-bounded read (slowloris
 *     mitigation, NET-9.6); a non-positive `timeoutMs` is the unbounded
 *     [`readAsync`](#readAsync).
 *   - [`close`](#close) releases the channel (the socket, and for TLS a
 *     clean `close_notify` first). Idempotent.
 *
 * The `offset` / `length` are `int64` to match the socket surface; in
 * practice the buffered reader/writer always pass `offset == 0`.
 */
public interface ByteChannel {
    /**
     * Read up to `length` bytes into `buf[offset .. offset+length)`,
     * parking the fiber until data arrives. Returns the count read
     * (`>= 0`); `0` is EOF.
     */
    int64 readAsync(int8[] buf, int64 offset, int64 length);

    /**
     * Write **all** of `data[offset .. offset+length)`, parking the fiber
     * on back-pressure until every byte is accepted (or a write raises).
     */
    void writeAllAsync(int8[] data, int64 offset, int64 length);

    /**
     * Deadline-bounded [`readAsync`](#readAsync): bound the total wait by
     * `timeoutMs` milliseconds, raising
     * [`TimedOutException`](TimedOutException.cajeta) if the deadline
     * elapses with the peer idle. A non-positive `timeoutMs` is the
     * unbounded [`readAsync`](#readAsync).
     */
    int64 readWithin(int8[] buf, int64 offset, int64 length, int32 timeoutMs);

    /** Release the channel (idempotent). */
    void close();
}
